{
  "title": "KYC integrations: the data you shouldn't store",
  "content": "There's a one-line change buried in most kyc identity setups that nobody flags until it's expensive. The dashboards look healthy right up until the incident starts. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke.\n\nMost teams integrating KYC treat it like any other third-party API: send data, get a verdict, store the response. That instinct is the problem. The contrarian take here is that the default architecture — your backend calling the vendor directly and persisting the full identity payload — is almost always wrong for European deployments. The right pattern is to route verification through a component that never lets raw identity documents or full identity profiles settle in your primary datastore, and to treat the vendor's verdict as a derived, time-bounded fact rather than a durable record. If your current integration stores passport scans, selfie images, or full name+address+DOB tuples in your own database \"just in case\", you are carrying GDPR Article 9-adjacent risk and audit burden you do not need, and you are probably doing it because the vendor SDK made it the path of least resistance.\n\n## The stance: verification is a data-minimisation problem before it is an API problem\n\nKYC vendors sell identity verification. They do not sell data custody advice, and their SDKs are optimised for successful verification, not for minimising what you keep. A typical integration looks like this: the frontend posts a document image to your API, your API forwards it to the vendor, the vendor returns a JSON blob with extracted fields, confidence scores, document images, and a decision, and your API writes that blob to a `kyc_verifications` table. That table then becomes a permanent, queryable copy of identity documents and personal data, subject to subject access requests, erasure requests, breach notification, and retention limits you now have to enforce yourself.\n\nI think the more interesting question is not \"which KYC vendor has the best pass rate\" but \"what is the minimum set of facts my system needs to retain after the vendor has made a decision\". For most products the answer is: a boolean outcome, a timestamp, an expiry, a reference to the vendor's case ID, and the level of assurance achieved. Everything else — the document image, the extracted MRZ, the address history, the liveness score — can stay with the vendor, who is contractually and operationally set up to hold it. Your database should hold a pointer, not a copy.\n\nThis is not a theoretical purity argument. It changes your architecture: it means the verification flow is a short-lived, isolated pipeline with its own storage rules, not a feature bolted onto your main user table. It means your main application database never sees a passport number. And it means your audit trail is built from events and references, not from retained personal data.\n\n## Why the \"store everything for audit\" advice falls short\n\nThe common advice is that you must retain identity data for audit and AML record-keeping, so you store the full vendor response. That advice is half right and dangerously incomplete. AML record-keeping obligations are real: under the EU Anti-Money Laundering Directive framework, obliged entities must retain customer due diligence records for a defined period after the end of the business relationship — commonly five years, with member-state variation. If you are an obliged entity, you cannot simply delete everything after verification.\n\nBut here is the distinction that gets lost: the obligation to be able to demonstrate that CDD was performed is not the same as the obligation to store the raw evidence in your primary operational database. The vendor is typically the record-holder for the underlying evidence, and your obligation can be satisfied by retaining a verifiable reference plus the decision metadata, with contractual guarantees that the vendor retains the underlying records for the required period and can produce them on request. Where you genuinely must retain copies, that retention belongs in a separate, access-controlled store with its own retention schedule and deletion jobs — not in the `users` table where every analytics query and every support tool can reach it.\n\nThe second half of the common advice — \"just encrypt it\" — also falls short. Encryption at rest protects against some breach scenarios but does nothing for subject access requests, erasure requests, or the operational fact that a support engineer with database read access can see identity documents. GDPR Article 5(1)(c) data minimisation and Article 5(1)(e) storage limitation are about not holding the data in the first place, not about holding it securely. A field-level encrypted passport number in your main database is still personal data you have to find, export, and delete on request.\n\n## The better mental model: a verification sidecar with a narrow contract\n\nThe architecture I would argue for is a verification sidecar: a small service (or a well-isolated module with its own database schema and credentials) whose only job is to talk to the KYC vendor and emit a minimal, signed result. Your main application never calls the vendor directly and never receives raw identity payloads. The sidecar has a narrow contract with the rest of the system: given a user reference and a verification session, return an outcome record containing only what downstream systems need.\n\nA minimal outcome record looks like this:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"user_ref\": \"u_8f3a...\",\n  \"vendor\": \"vendor_name\",\n  \"vendor_case_id\": \"case_123...\",\n  \"outcome\": \"verified\",\n  \"assurance_level\": \"document_and_liveness\",\n  \"decided_at\": \"2026-02-11T09:14:00Z\",\n  \"expires_at\": \"2027-02-11T00:00:00Z\",\n  \"evidence_retained_by_vendor\": true,\n  \"schema_version\": 1\n}\n```\n\nNote what is absent: name, date of birth, address, document number, document image, extracted fields. If your product genuinely needs the verified legal name — for example to print it on a contract — that is a separate, justified data flow with its own lawful basis and retention rule, and it should be modelled as such rather than smuggled in as a side effect of KYC. The sidecar's database holds the outcome records and an append-only event log of decisions; it does not hold the evidence.\n\nThe sidecar pattern also gives you a clean place to enforce the things that are easy to forget when KYC is just another endpoint in your monolith:\n\n- **Network egress control.** Only the sidecar can reach the vendor's API. Your main application's egress rules and service credentials do not include the vendor.\n- **Retention and deletion.** The sidecar owns the retention schedule for outcome records and the deletion job that runs when a user exercises erasure. Deleting an outcome record does not require touching the vendor's evidence store if the contract says the vendor handles erasure propagation.\n- **Audit events.** Decisions are emitted as events (user reference, outcome, timestamp, vendor case reference) to your audit log. The audit log contains no special-category data, so it can be retained and queried more freely.\n- **Vendor swap.** Because the contract is a small outcome schema, replacing the vendor is a sidecar change, not a schema migration across your product.\n\n### Where the raw data actually goes\n\nIn a web-based flow, the cleanest arrangement is for the document capture and liveness steps to happen directly between the user's browser and the vendor's hosted flow, so the raw images never transit your servers at all. Many vendors support this with a hosted session or a tokenised SDK flow. If you must proxy the capture through your backend — for example because the vendor's flow does not support your region or your CSP — then the proxying component should be the sidecar, streaming the payload to the vendor without writing it to disk, and the sidecar should have no persistent volume for request bodies.\n\nThis is where a lot of integrations quietly go wrong: the sidecar is written as a normal web service, request logging is left on, and document images end up in an access log or an error-tracking payload. If you take one thing from this piece, take this: turn off request body logging on the verification path, and check your error tracker's scrubbing rules before you ship.\n\n## Where the common advice is still right\n\nI am not arguing that you should never store identity data. There are cases where the sidecar-plus-reference model is not sufficient:\n\n- **You are the obliged entity and your regulator expects you to hold the evidence.** In that case, hold it — but in a dedicated evidence store with its own access controls, encryption keys, retention schedule, and deletion tooling, not in your product database. The sidecar pattern still applies; it just gains an evidence store behind it.\n- **Offline or low-connectivity verification.** If verification happens where the vendor cannot be reached in real time and the decision must be made locally, you will hold data locally. Isolate it and delete it as soon as the decision is recorded and synced.\n- **Re-verification and periodic review.** AML frameworks require ongoing monitoring and periodic refresh of CDD. This is an argument for keeping a durable reference and a re-verification schedule, not for keeping the original document image forever.\n\nAnd the common advice to use a reputable vendor rather than building verification in-house is right. Document forensics, liveness detection, and sanctions screening are specialist problems. The architectural argument here is about what you keep, not about whether to use a vendor.\n\n## A decision rule for what to persist\n\nWhen a new field arrives from the vendor, the default should be \"do not persist\". Persist a field only if you can answer all three questions affirmatively:\n\n1. **Does a downstream system need this field to function?** Not \"might be useful for support\" — actually needs it, and cannot fetch it from the vendor on demand.\n2. **Is there a documented legal or contractual basis to retain it, with a defined retention period?** \"Audit\" is not an answer unless you can name the obligation and the period.\n3. **Can you delete it on request without breaking that downstream system?** If deletion would break a process, the field is load-bearing and you need a design that does not depend on it.\n\nA useful corollary: any field that fails these questions but that you still want for analytics should be aggregated or hashed at ingest, not stored raw. Counts of verification outcomes by day and country are useful; a table of verified names is a liability.\n\n| Data item | Default persistence | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Outcome (verified / rejected / pending) | Yes | Sidecar outcome table |\n| Vendor case ID | Yes | Sidecar outcome table |\n| Decision timestamp and expiry | Yes | Sidecar outcome table |\n| Assurance level | Yes | Sidecar outcome table |\n| Verified legal name | Only if a product flow needs it | Separate justified store |\n| Document number | No | Vendor only |\n| Document image / selfie | No | Vendor only |\n| Extracted address | No unless required | Separate justified store |\n| Liveness / confidence scores | No | Vendor only |\n\nThis table is a starting position, not a universal answer; the point is that every \"Yes\" should be defensible in writing.\n\n## The integration details that bite\n\nA few concrete failure modes show up repeatedly when teams wire KYC into a European stack.\n\n**Webhook payloads carry more than you think.** Vendor webhooks often include the full decision payload, not just the outcome. If your webhook handler writes the raw body to your event bus or a `webhook_events` table for replay, you have just recreated the data store you were trying to avoid. Parse the payload, extract the minimal outcome, and discard the rest — or route the webhook to the sidecar only.\n\n**Idempotency keys and replay.** Verification decisions can be delivered more than once. Your sidecar should treat the vendor case ID as the idempotency key and make outcome writes upserts, so a replayed webhook does not create duplicate records or, worse, a second copy of the payload in a dead-letter queue.\n\n**Retention jobs that never run.** A retention policy that exists only in a document is not a control. The sidecar should have a scheduled job that deletes outcome records past their retention period and emits a count to your metrics. If that count is zero for a month, either nothing is expiring (suspicious) or the job is broken.\n\n**Erasure requests that stop at your database.** When a user exercises the right to erasure, your deletion job must cover the sidecar outcome table and must trigger the vendor's erasure process for the evidence they hold. This needs to be in the vendor contract and in your runbook, not discovered during the request.\n\n**Region and transfer.** If your vendor processes data outside the EEA, the transfer mechanism (for example Standard Contractual Clauses with a transfer impact assessment) is part of the integration, and your data protection impact assessment should cover the verification flow specifically. A DPIA that says \"we use a KYC vendor\" without describing what data leaves, where it goes, and what you retain is not doing the job.\n\n**Logging and error tracking.** As above: request bodies, response bodies, and error contexts on the verification path must be scrubbed. This is the single most common way raw identity data leaks into systems that were never designed to hold it.\n\n### A minimal sidecar handler\n\nThe following is a self-contained illustration of the shape, not a production service. It shows the contract: accept a minimal start request, call the vendor, persist only the outcome, and return only the outcome.\n\n```python\n# Illustrative only. Assumes a vendor client and a database session.\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\n\nOUTCOME_FIELDS = {\"user_ref\", \"vendor\", \"vendor_case_id\", \"outcome\",\n                  \"assurance_level\", \"decided_at\", \"expires_at\", \"schema_version\"}\n\ndef record_outcome(db, user_ref: str, vendor_payload: dict) -> dict:\n    # Extract only the fields we are allowed to keep. Everything else is dropped.\n    outcome = {\n        \"user_ref\": user_ref,\n        \"vendor\": \"vendor_name\",\n        \"vendor_case_id\": vendor_payload[\"case_id\"],\n        \"outcome\": vendor_payload[\"decision\"],\n        \"assurance_level\": vendor_payload.get(\"assurance\", \"unknown\"),\n        \"decided_at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc),\n        \"expires_at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc) + timedelta(days=365),\n        \"schema_version\": 1,\n    }\n    # Upsert on vendor_case_id so replays are harmless.\n    db.execute(\n        \"\"\"INSERT INTO kyc_outcomes (user_ref, vendor, vendor_case_id, outcome,\n               assurance_level, decided_at, expires_at, schema_version)\n           VALUES (:user_ref, :vendor, :vendor_case_id, :outcome,\n               :assurance_level, :decided_at, :expires_at, :schema_version)\n           ON CONFLICT (vendor_case_id) DO UPDATE SET\n               outcome = EXCLUDED.outcome,\n               assurance_level = EXCLUDED.assurance_level,\n               decided_at = EXCLUDED.decided_at,\n               expires_at = EXCLUDED.expires_at\"\"\",\n        outcome,\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    # Emit an audit event with no special-category data.\n    db.execute(\n        \"INSERT INTO audit_events (event_type, subject_ref, occurred_at, detail)\"\n        \" VALUES ('kyc.decision', :user_ref, :decided_at, :outcome)\",\n        {\"user_ref\": user_ref, \"decided_at\": outcome[\"decided_at\"],\n         \"outcome\": outcome[\"outcome\"]},\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    return {k: outcome[k] for k in OUTCOME_FIELDS}\n```\n\nThe important line is the one that builds `outcome` from a small allow-list. If a new field appears in the vendor payload, it is ignored until someone deliberately adds it and records why.\n\n## What to check in the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your verification integration's webhook handler and your sidecar's request logging configuration, and search your codebase for where the vendor's response body is written to a table, a log, or an error tracker. If you find a `raw_response` column or a `logger.info(response.json())` on the verification path, that is your first fix: replace it with an allow-list extraction like the one above, and add a scheduled deletion job for any historical rows that already contain raw identity data. That single change moves you from \"we store identity documents and hope\" to \"we store a decision and a pointer\", which is the architectural position every other compliance control depends on.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Stripe: idempotent requests](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests)\n- [NIST SP 800-92: Guide to Computer Security Log Management](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
